WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.609 --> 00:00:06.119
Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I'm Rhett.

00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:07.259
And I'm Link.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:09.820
This week, at my dining table

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.443
and the illustrious card
table in the creative house

00:00:13.443 --> 00:00:15.689
where Rhett, I see you're sitting.

00:00:15.689 --> 00:00:17.226
Yes, once again.

00:00:17.226 --> 00:00:18.726
Yes, once again.

00:00:19.656 --> 00:00:21.382
We're gonna dive into

00:00:21.382 --> 00:00:24.495
a number of topics that you have dictated

00:00:24.495 --> 00:00:28.179
in response to a prompt that we put out

00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:29.836
across the universe.

00:00:29.836 --> 00:00:32.614
I mean, when you put prompts out there,

00:00:32.614 --> 00:00:36.232
it's like, hey guys, do you
have any questions for us?

00:00:36.232 --> 00:00:39.392
I wonder if aliens are
like tempted to respond.

00:00:39.392 --> 00:00:43.329
You know, it's like if there
are aliens with a capacity

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:45.003
to monitor everything we're doing.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:46.878
Are they like man, I
really want to respond.

00:00:46.878 --> 00:00:48.918
And if they do, could
they be using an alias

00:00:48.918 --> 00:00:49.896
as one of these people?

00:00:49.896 --> 00:00:51.494
I don't know, we can assess that.

00:00:51.494 --> 00:00:55.219
Well, from a physical
limitations standpoint,

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:58.437
if they wanted to respond, I mean,

00:00:58.437 --> 00:01:01.941
the best technology that
we have at this point

00:01:01.941 --> 00:01:04.509
can only move information
at the speed of light.

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:08.597
So if they do respond, it's
gonna be years from now

00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:10.761
and it will be irrelevant.

00:01:10.761 --> 00:01:12.267
Now if they are here.

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.607
Oh, you're assuming

00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:16.831
that they have the
limitations that we have.

00:01:16.831 --> 00:01:18.784
Well, no, I mean, they may be here

00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:20.086
in some form or fashion,

00:01:20.086 --> 00:01:22.858
or they may have a way to
monitor us even from a distance

00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:24.206
in a way that we don't.

00:01:24.206 --> 00:01:25.068
So in that case,

00:01:25.068 --> 00:01:26.176
they could respond.
They could also

00:01:26.176 --> 00:01:28.881
communicate with us,
yeah, instantaneously.

00:01:28.881 --> 00:01:32.244
My mind's really, in the world of aliens,

00:01:32.244 --> 00:01:37.244
because even though I read
"The Three-Body Problem",

00:01:37.314 --> 00:01:39.231
then I read the sequel,

00:01:41.905 --> 00:01:43.820
"Dark Forest",

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.121
and then I was like, you know what?

00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:47.896
This is too bleak and it's too heady

00:01:47.896 --> 00:01:49.695
and it's too much, I can't do it.

00:01:49.695 --> 00:01:51.933
And then I realized that
I've been paying for Audible

00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:54.870
and I'd forgotten that I should
listen to some audio books.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.620
You said that you listened to books

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.945
while you you're walking
around the neighborhood.

00:01:58.945 --> 00:02:00.453
And I was like, I should give that a try.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.216
And so I downloaded the third book,

00:02:03.216 --> 00:02:05.338
the final book in the trilogy.

00:02:05.338 --> 00:02:07.705
I've been listening to that.

00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:11.742
It may, I mean, I can
walk for a long time.

00:02:11.742 --> 00:02:13.534
Like I'm walking like over an hour

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:16.997
'cause I'm just walking as
a way to listen to a book.

00:02:16.997 --> 00:02:18.549
I don't even know where I am.

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:20.214
Yeah and you get so many more books

00:02:20.214 --> 00:02:22.901
and I use the term read, I don't care.

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:25.296
When somebody says, I don't specify,

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:26.762
I'm listening to a book.

00:02:26.762 --> 00:02:31.262
I'm like, I'm reading a book
because I mean, who am I?

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.049
I'm not a guy that listens to books.

00:02:36.046 --> 00:02:37.229
No, actually I am.

00:02:37.229 --> 00:02:39.757
And I've gotten through so
many more books than normal

00:02:39.757 --> 00:02:42.122
just because I've been walking so much.

00:02:42.122 --> 00:02:46.565
Do you walk slower or faster
when it gets more intense

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:49.052
or is it irrespective?

00:02:49.052 --> 00:02:53.207
Irrespective, I walk more
like what you're saying,

00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:54.829
when I want to keep listening.

00:02:54.829 --> 00:02:59.318
In fact, last night I finished
a book that was great.

00:02:59.318 --> 00:03:02.877
And this one took me like five
days of walking to finish.

00:03:02.877 --> 00:03:04.310
What book?

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.381
So it was a pretty short book.

00:03:06.381 --> 00:03:07.765
It's called, "The Stranger in the Woods."

00:03:07.765 --> 00:03:09.764
You remember we did that,

00:03:09.764 --> 00:03:12.537
I would have made this a
wreck in a subsequent episode,

00:03:12.537 --> 00:03:15.370
so now you're stealing it from me.

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:20.382
We did that episode on GMM about hermits

00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:23.578
or weird people, I don't know what it was,

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:24.596
it was back in the day

00:03:24.596 --> 00:03:26.784
and we talked about the North Pond Hermit,

00:03:26.784 --> 00:03:30.451
the guy who lived next
to that lake in Maine

00:03:31.838 --> 00:03:36.231
and was isolated, by himself,
with no human contact--

00:03:36.231 --> 00:03:38.371
That is what isolation means.

00:03:38.371 --> 00:03:39.428
Except for one--

00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:41.223
You could be isolated
with people, I guess.

00:03:41.223 --> 00:03:44.185
Except for one interaction in 27 years

00:03:44.185 --> 00:03:46.741
and he's thought to be a person who was

00:03:46.741 --> 00:03:49.920
the most isolated person in
human history potentially.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.744
Which I think is what we titled the video

00:03:51.744 --> 00:03:54.345
back when we did those types of videos.

00:03:54.345 --> 00:03:57.472
It's an incredible story of a journalist

00:03:57.472 --> 00:03:59.921
who wanted to--
Track him down?

00:03:59.921 --> 00:04:00.976
Interact with this guy.

00:04:00.976 --> 00:04:01.916
No, no, no.

00:04:01.916 --> 00:04:05.011
It's after he got arrested
again and, you know,

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.763
because he was stealing from everybody.

00:04:06.763 --> 00:04:07.818
Right.

00:04:07.818 --> 00:04:10.956
I was like, why is this
book rated so highly?

00:04:10.956 --> 00:04:14.373
And then I, well I learned by reading it,

00:04:15.285 --> 00:04:17.244
AKA listening to it

00:04:17.244 --> 00:04:21.413
because it's an incredibly introspective,

00:04:21.413 --> 00:04:23.532
like the journalist is very introspective

00:04:23.532 --> 00:04:27.876
and the way that he explores
the concept of isolation.

00:04:27.876 --> 00:04:30.804
And hermitage, if that's a word.

00:04:30.804 --> 00:04:31.966
Hmm.

00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:33.869
It's only like a six hour listen--

00:04:33.869 --> 00:04:35.432
And it's a good time to read it.

00:04:35.432 --> 00:04:40.432
When you're trying to gain
some sort of, what's the word--

00:04:40.992 --> 00:04:42.351
Perspective?

00:04:42.351 --> 00:04:44.714
No, like a connection to this guy

00:04:44.714 --> 00:04:46.895
who's done this there's comradery.

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:49.004
Like, hey, I'm not
alone in these feelings.

00:04:49.004 --> 00:04:51.212
His level of isolation,

00:04:51.212 --> 00:04:54.313
the funny thing is it not at
one point during the book,

00:04:54.313 --> 00:04:56.361
did it strike me that I was doing

00:04:56.361 --> 00:04:59.685
anything remotely close to
what this guy was doing.

00:04:59.685 --> 00:05:02.451
Did that help or was it, didn't matter?

00:05:02.451 --> 00:05:04.125
No, it didn't matter.

00:05:04.125 --> 00:05:06.582
It's just, it's touching.

00:05:06.582 --> 00:05:07.832
In fact.
Wow.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:13.900
At the end, like I'd walked
for four and a half miles

00:05:14.062 --> 00:05:16.788
last night listening to this book

00:05:16.788 --> 00:05:18.991
and I was coming up to my house

00:05:18.991 --> 00:05:21.105
and I was like, I don't really want to,

00:05:21.105 --> 00:05:23.842
I can look at my I'm listening
to the book on my watch.

00:05:23.842 --> 00:05:25.411
I don't even have my
phone, it's just my watch

00:05:25.411 --> 00:05:26.911
and my headphones.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.947
And I'm like, there's 15 minutes left,

00:05:32.909 --> 00:05:35.576
I went and sat on my front steps

00:05:36.438 --> 00:05:37.981
and then I just began to weep.

00:05:38.864 --> 00:05:39.851
Really?

00:05:39.851 --> 00:05:42.768
There's a moment at the end where--

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.411
Oh, don't spoil it.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:48.998
Where it will make you
cry if you have a heart.

00:05:48.998 --> 00:05:50.209
What do you mean?

00:05:50.209 --> 00:05:52.123
You don't know if I have a heart?

00:05:52.123 --> 00:05:53.959
I'm just saying for all
the people out there,

00:05:53.959 --> 00:05:55.905
not everybody cries at everything.

00:05:55.905 --> 00:05:57.930
Rhett, no one's listening
to this conversation.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.813
It's just me and you, man.

00:05:59.813 --> 00:06:00.646
And I do have--

00:06:00.646 --> 00:06:01.480
I didn't plan on talking about that.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.972
I was gonna make it a rec for later

00:06:03.972 --> 00:06:06.326
because "The Stranger in the Woods",

00:06:06.326 --> 00:06:08.368
"The Stranger in the
Woods", I think it's called.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:11.035
So where do you land right now

00:06:13.112 --> 00:06:15.695
on podcasts versus audio books.

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:20.275
And how are you navigating that?

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:22.031
'Cause I know you like
listening to podcasts too

00:06:22.031 --> 00:06:24.776
and if you get on a good series?

00:06:24.776 --> 00:06:25.874
Yeah.

00:06:25.874 --> 00:06:26.707
And see for me,

00:06:26.707 --> 00:06:28.260
my thing is always about like,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.856
am I gonna listen to music?

00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:33.286
'Cause I'm, you know, or am
I gonna listen to a podcast?

00:06:33.286 --> 00:06:35.222
And now am I gonna listen to an audio?

00:06:35.222 --> 00:06:38.651
I think audio book has
taken the place of podcasts

00:06:38.651 --> 00:06:40.484
for me at this moment.

00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:42.332
Yeah.

00:06:42.332 --> 00:06:44.487
Well it, you know,

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:48.148
for some reason, because
I listen to podcasts a lot

00:06:48.148 --> 00:06:50.465
and I listen to them,
especially while driving.

00:06:50.465 --> 00:06:53.113
I was already listening
to a lot of audio books,

00:06:53.113 --> 00:06:55.898
but slowly audio books have
sort of pushed podcast out.

00:06:55.898 --> 00:07:00.898
But it's an interesting dynamic
because I also listen to,

00:07:01.071 --> 00:07:02.456
I don't listen to music when I work out.

00:07:02.456 --> 00:07:03.474
Most people do.

00:07:03.474 --> 00:07:05.755
Most people need some kind of music

00:07:05.755 --> 00:07:06.975
and I understand that--

00:07:06.975 --> 00:07:08.200
Some BPM. baby.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.867
But I'm just like,

00:07:11.331 --> 00:07:15.566
I kind of want what I get from
the book or from the podcast

00:07:15.566 --> 00:07:18.133
more than I want the feeling
that the music gives me

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:20.458
when I'm working out

00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:23.265
and some books can get too complex

00:07:23.265 --> 00:07:24.937
to listen to while you're like.

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:30.776
Well, you have to be present.

00:07:30.776 --> 00:07:33.718
I mean, you have to be
embodied to really work out

00:07:33.718 --> 00:07:36.953
'cause as opposed to walking.

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:39.717
I think that it could be dangerous.

00:07:39.717 --> 00:07:41.345
But I do both.

00:07:41.345 --> 00:07:44.283
I still do more audio book listening

00:07:44.283 --> 00:07:47.456
while working out than podcasts,

00:07:47.456 --> 00:07:48.518
but I find that podcast

00:07:48.518 --> 00:07:51.327
because there sort of a
lighthearted conversation

00:07:51.327 --> 00:07:52.584
most of the time,

00:07:52.584 --> 00:07:53.851
you can kind of tune in and tune out

00:07:53.851 --> 00:07:55.975
and it is better for working out, but--

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.437
I can't count to 10,

00:07:57.437 --> 00:07:59.030
like I couldn't count reps

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.230
and listen to the book I'm listening to.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.487
I mean, it's a very complex book

00:08:04.487 --> 00:08:07.511
and I'm not that complex of a guy,

00:08:07.511 --> 00:08:09.753
so I really gotta apply myself.

00:08:09.753 --> 00:08:14.318
If I had to count to 10,
three times in a row,

00:08:14.318 --> 00:08:15.945
I couldn't do it.

00:08:15.945 --> 00:08:17.727
Which reminds me of meditation.

00:08:17.727 --> 00:08:20.623
There's a thing in
meditation where it's like,

00:08:20.623 --> 00:08:22.507
you count your breaths
and when you get to 10,

00:08:22.507 --> 00:08:23.757
you start over,

00:08:25.185 --> 00:08:27.651
most of the time I don't ever get to 10,

00:08:27.651 --> 00:08:29.327
the first time.

00:08:29.327 --> 00:08:30.512
You know?

00:08:30.512 --> 00:08:31.345
I definitely couldn't--

00:08:31.345 --> 00:08:32.178
Yeah, it's very difficult.

00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:34.721
I don't want to be trying
to do my reps, anyway.

00:08:34.721 --> 00:08:36.392
Or sometimes I'll find myself in the teens

00:08:36.392 --> 00:08:38.052
and I'll be like, oh, you're
supposed to start over.

00:08:39.942 --> 00:08:41.196
Yeah.
That happens sometimes too,

00:08:41.196 --> 00:08:43.808
Because you can count on autopilot unless

00:08:43.808 --> 00:08:45.774
you're doing bench presses.
Two quick things though.

00:08:45.774 --> 00:08:47.384
Two quick things before
we get into the real deal.

00:08:47.384 --> 00:08:48.815
Take all the time you need.

00:08:48.815 --> 00:08:50.938
First thing is I want
to acknowledge the fact

00:08:50.938 --> 00:08:52.938
that this is very likely

00:08:55.746 --> 00:08:58.032
unforeseeable circumstances
not withstanding.

00:08:58.032 --> 00:08:59.448
Fingers crossed.

00:08:59.448 --> 00:09:02.019
That we will do the
podcast separate, right?

00:09:02.019 --> 00:09:03.441
So we actually--

00:09:03.441 --> 00:09:04.486
The last split screen podcast.

00:09:04.486 --> 00:09:07.024
We can break this to you, Ear Biscuiteer,

00:09:07.024 --> 00:09:09.513
because you're gonna
find out tomorrow anyway

00:09:09.513 --> 00:09:11.307
if you listen to this when it comes out,

00:09:11.307 --> 00:09:13.368
but we actually went back into the studio,

00:09:13.368 --> 00:09:14.201
just the two of us--
No, no,

00:09:14.201 --> 00:09:16.265
this comes out Monday,

00:09:16.265 --> 00:09:20.055
along with the episode,
they're at the same time.

00:09:20.055 --> 00:09:21.857
Oh, yeah, on Monday, not on Sunday.

00:09:21.857 --> 00:09:24.022
So, no one's finding anything out.

00:09:24.022 --> 00:09:26.458
Yeah, so the episode of GMM

00:09:26.458 --> 00:09:28.825
that you could watch today,

00:09:28.825 --> 00:09:32.063
if you're listening to this
on Monday when it comes out,

00:09:32.063 --> 00:09:33.275
you'll see that it's just,

00:09:33.275 --> 00:09:36.291
it's me and Link and Stevie in the studio,

00:09:36.291 --> 00:09:38.726
because the three of us
have been in quarantine

00:09:38.726 --> 00:09:40.278
and we have determined
that it is safe for us

00:09:40.278 --> 00:09:43.385
to get together and
shoot the show together

00:09:43.385 --> 00:09:44.959
and that's the way we're gonna do it

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:48.012
