But five cam for like our traditional

00:11:51.603 --> 00:11:54.698
five camera set up
running at the same time,

00:11:54.698 --> 00:11:56.980
our audio feed coming from the mic

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:58.941
and being monitored by Chris,

00:11:58.941 --> 00:12:01.352
our audio guy, who was
watching a video feed

00:12:01.352 --> 00:12:06.352
of just the wave forms or
just the audio kit basically.

00:12:06.421 --> 00:12:08.039
It was an incredible system and--

00:12:08.039 --> 00:12:09.363
And it was demanding.
We sent it to the crew.

00:12:09.363 --> 00:12:12.613
We sent it to the crew, but it was like

00:12:15.252 --> 00:12:16.823
one of the things that
we've said multiple times

00:12:16.823 --> 00:12:19.309
is how easy they make it for
us when we're in the studio.

00:12:19.309 --> 00:12:21.447
Well, they actually made it easy for us

00:12:21.447 --> 00:12:22.562
when they weren't in the studio.

00:12:22.562 --> 00:12:24.644
Now, there was a lot of like moving parts

00:12:24.644 --> 00:12:25.627
and cleaning up and stuff

00:12:25.627 --> 00:12:27.752
that we normally don't
have to worry about.

00:12:27.752 --> 00:12:28.585
Yeah.

00:12:28.585 --> 00:12:32.109
But it was still, I mean,
you'll be able to see

00:12:32.109 --> 00:12:34.962
when you see these episodes,
next week's episodes,

00:12:34.962 --> 00:12:36.731
which is still just three a week for now.

00:12:36.731 --> 00:12:38.403
You can say today's episode.

00:12:38.403 --> 00:12:40.736
Yeah, this week's episode.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:43.210
There's a joy.
We were having a good time.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:44.831
Yeah, there's a palpable joy--

00:12:44.831 --> 00:12:45.729
Having a good time.

00:12:45.729 --> 00:12:48.300
I was so relieved that the system worked

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.317
and then we got, you know,

00:12:49.317 --> 00:12:51.083
there was a couple of
hitches at the beginning

00:12:51.083 --> 00:12:52.389
with getting everything booted up

00:12:52.389 --> 00:12:53.778
and all the cameras to wake up.

00:12:53.778 --> 00:12:57.569
But then, and there were
there creative limitations

00:12:57.569 --> 00:13:00.736
when it's just the three of us there.

00:13:00.736 --> 00:13:03.938
I'm so appreciative to the extended team

00:13:03.938 --> 00:13:07.660
and I mean, you'll be
able to see the difference

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.660
in this week's episodes.

00:13:10.934 --> 00:13:12.768
And again, it's still just three of them.

00:13:12.768 --> 00:13:14.947
We're gonna go to five as soon as we can,

00:13:14.947 --> 00:13:19.864
but that's not this week as
you're listening to this fresh.

00:13:22.221 --> 00:13:24.704
But yeah, it was great to be in the space

00:13:24.704 --> 00:13:27.597
and afterwards the thing that
I Slacked out to the team

00:13:27.597 --> 00:13:29.121
and that I was reflecting.

00:13:29.121 --> 00:13:32.386
It's funny because as I
was driving home yesterday,

00:13:32.386 --> 00:13:35.572
having recorded the
episodes in the studio,

00:13:35.572 --> 00:13:38.017
I started listening to the audio book

00:13:38.017 --> 00:13:39.727
because I'm so hooked on this story,

00:13:39.727 --> 00:13:41.794
I just want to keep going,

00:13:41.794 --> 00:13:44.404
but then I was like, you know what?

00:13:44.404 --> 00:13:45.678
I'm gonna pause this

00:13:45.678 --> 00:13:50.250
and I'm gonna reflect
on what happened today.

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:52.536
And so on the ride home,

00:13:52.536 --> 00:13:53.369
which was a lot shorter,

00:13:53.369 --> 00:13:56.175
'cause ain't nobody on the freeway still.

00:13:56.175 --> 00:14:00.175
I just took that time
just to stop and celebrate

00:14:02.297 --> 00:14:03.634
the fact that we did it.

00:14:03.634 --> 00:14:05.183
I mean, so by the time I got home,

00:14:05.183 --> 00:14:09.920
I was able to convey what I
appreciated myself to the team

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.003
and that was,

00:14:13.513 --> 00:14:17.090
it was the experience
of being behind the desk

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.224
and for us to like be in the same space

00:14:19.224 --> 00:14:23.141
and to make the show and
it was the most normal

00:14:24.033 --> 00:14:27.946
that I felt since being in quarantine.

00:14:27.946 --> 00:14:29.613
I mean, it was just,

00:14:32.286 --> 00:14:34.154
it was refreshing, it was relieving,

00:14:34.154 --> 00:14:38.181
it was like a sigh of
relief to drive into work

00:14:38.181 --> 00:14:41.425
and to do it and then to drive back home.

00:14:41.425 --> 00:14:43.842
It felt so like it used to be

00:14:45.349 --> 00:14:46.896
and then when I got home,

00:14:46.896 --> 00:14:47.956
I entered back into a space

00:14:47.956 --> 00:14:50.282
where no one else was feeling that

00:14:50.282 --> 00:14:53.532
and we're navigating all the challenges

00:14:54.409 --> 00:14:57.076
of the accumulation of isolation

00:14:59.887 --> 00:15:04.162
and frustration, and how
all that comes out sideways.

00:15:04.162 --> 00:15:06.478
But I had more in my tank

00:15:06.478 --> 00:15:09.167
than I've had in a really long time,

00:15:09.167 --> 00:15:12.865
because just of the sense
of normalcy is so powerful.

00:15:12.865 --> 00:15:15.294
Well, it's funny you talk
about that transition

00:15:15.294 --> 00:15:18.946
from basically going to
work for the first time.

00:15:18.946 --> 00:15:20.086
I mean, we've been working,

00:15:20.086 --> 00:15:24.330
but the whole idea of
going into the studio,

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:28.015
and getting back into that
rhythm of like I'm at this space

00:15:28.015 --> 00:15:32.365
where it is designated as
a place where I do my job.

00:15:32.365 --> 00:15:37.365
And then I come back to my home
and I was in the same place.

00:15:37.918 --> 00:15:39.915
I was feeling really good.

00:15:39.915 --> 00:15:42.570
I was very proud of our team.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:47.069
They show us all the time,
how good they are what they do

00:15:47.069 --> 00:15:50.122
and how they facilitate
what we're best at doing.

00:15:50.122 --> 00:15:52.780
They do what they're best at,

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:54.555
so that we can do what we're best at,

00:15:54.555 --> 00:15:56.221
and it works, right?
Hmm-mm.

00:15:56.221 --> 00:15:57.131
And yesterday,

00:15:57.131 --> 00:15:58.894
even though we had doubts
about this process,

00:15:58.894 --> 00:16:00.301
like what is it gonna be
like for just the three of us

00:16:00.301 --> 00:16:02.457
to be in there and not be remote.

00:16:02.457 --> 00:16:04.740
Well, they figured it out and it worked.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.907
And then we could have a good
time, which is the key to us

00:16:07.907 --> 00:16:10.636
making a good show is
us having a good time.

00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:14.194
So I was thinking the same
thing, and then I got home

00:16:14.194 --> 00:16:17.027
and we had experimented with this,

00:16:18.639 --> 00:16:23.222
we've had trouble getting
Shepherd to do his schoolwork

00:16:24.324 --> 00:16:28.494
without constant
interference or intervening.

00:16:28.494 --> 00:16:31.984
And so me and Sharon had
a conversation last week

00:16:31.984 --> 00:16:35.213
where we were like, we're
gonna try something new,

00:16:35.213 --> 00:16:37.598
even though this is like
the last week of school

00:16:37.598 --> 00:16:39.151
or the last two weeks of school,

00:16:39.151 --> 00:16:40.724
but I was like we're gonna try something

00:16:40.724 --> 00:16:43.290
and just see if we can get this,

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:46.542
we're gonna give you the opportunity

00:16:46.542 --> 00:16:50.332
to basically set your schedule
and get your work done

00:16:50.332 --> 00:16:52.672
and we're gonna check in
at the end of the day,

00:16:52.672 --> 00:16:56.136
and what we're trying to do
is we're trying to instill

00:16:56.136 --> 00:16:58.041
or establish trust with you

00:16:58.041 --> 00:17:00.023
so that then you can have freedom, right?

00:17:00.023 --> 00:17:04.583
'Cause no one is enjoying
this constant argument about,

00:17:04.583 --> 00:17:05.455
hey, are you doing your work?

00:17:05.455 --> 00:17:06.622
You doing your work?

00:17:06.622 --> 00:17:08.278
And this is especially
difficult for Jessie

00:17:08.278 --> 00:17:09.944
because she's the one who,

00:17:09.944 --> 00:17:12.098
I'm over here at the
creative house or whatever

00:17:12.098 --> 00:17:15.381
and she's dealing with it constantly.

00:17:15.381 --> 00:17:16.214
So,

00:17:17.875 --> 00:17:19.873
I get home and it's like, first of all,

00:17:19.873 --> 00:17:22.482
I'm gonna grill some
tuna steaks on the grill.

00:17:22.482 --> 00:17:25.152
That was my job for dinner,

00:17:25.152 --> 00:17:27.779
but I was also gonna check in on Shepherd

00:17:27.779 --> 00:17:28.884
and I check in on Shepherd,

00:17:28.884 --> 00:17:30.823
and he kind of did some stuff,

00:17:30.823 --> 00:17:32.250
but then he didn't fully do it

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.021
and then there was an argument
that broke out about that

00:17:35.021 --> 00:17:38.816
and then there was an argument
that broke out with Lock.

00:17:38.816 --> 00:17:42.251
I don't even remember what it was.

00:17:42.251 --> 00:17:45.235
I was in the kitchen and
Jessie was cutting something

00:17:45.235 --> 00:17:46.068
with a pair of scissors

00:17:46.068 --> 00:17:49.967
and then I was walking and she
turned around with scissors

00:17:49.967 --> 00:17:52.866
and I bumped into her and no one was hurt,

00:17:52.866 --> 00:17:53.970
but then she thought it was my fault,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:54.996
I thought it was her fault.

00:17:54.996 --> 00:17:55.942
We're yelling at each other

00:17:55.942 --> 00:17:57.247
and she's got a pair of scissors.

00:17:57.247 --> 00:17:58.080
Oh, gosh.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.223
And so basically with every family member,

00:18:00.223 --> 00:18:01.689
with the exception of Barbara,

00:18:01.689 --> 00:18:04.564
I don't think Barbara did
anything to piss me off.

00:18:04.564 --> 00:18:05.897
She rarely does.

00:18:06.882 --> 00:18:09.825
It was like, I had gone from this place

00:18:09.825 --> 00:18:11.825
where everyone was doing

00:18:14.482 --> 00:18:17.598
exactly what I needed them to do

00:18:17.598 --> 00:18:19.915
in order to make my life easier.

00:18:21.669 --> 00:18:25.581
To a place where no one was doing anything

00:18:25.581 --> 00:18:26.763
that made my life easy.

00:18:26.763 --> 00:18:29.212
It wasn't about me and my life being easy.

00:18:29.212 --> 00:18:31.982
It was about being a part of this family

00:18:31.982 --> 00:18:35.645
where everybody's got their own needs,

00:18:35.645 --> 00:18:36.703
everybody's got their own stuff

00:18:36.703 --> 00:18:38.045
that they're dealing with

00:18:38.045 --> 00:18:40.533
and we're all tired of each other.

00:18:40.533 --> 00:18:44.200
And it was just like I
went from zero to 11,

00:18:47.475 --> 00:18:48.707
just all of a sudden.

00:18:48.707 --> 00:18:50.899
And then after dinner, Jessie was like,

00:18:53.684 --> 00:18:56.202
she said, "Do you want to go sleep

00:18:56.202 --> 00:18:58.576
"at the creative house tonight?"

00:18:58.576 --> 00:19:00.879
Oh, see that bed that I've put in there

00:19:00.879 --> 00:19:03.448
that you've been giving
me a hard time about.

00:19:03.448 --> 00:19:04.318
Now it's a pretty good idea.
Well, it was funny,

00:19:04.318 --> 00:19:06.719
because she there is a bed.

00:19:06.719 --> 00:19:08.097
Yeah.
I was like,

00:19:08.097 --> 00:19:09.680
it's a child's bed.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.715
Do I look like a child?

00:19:13.715 --> 00:19:15.590
It's not a child's bed, it's a twin bed.

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:17.744
So did you sleep on it, how did it feel?

00:19:17.744 --> 00:19:18.881
Well, I didn't sleep on it.

00:19:18.881 --> 00:19:20.961
I said, "I'm gonna do that."

00:19:20.961 --> 00:19:23.794
I said, "I just need a little time

00:19:24.949 --> 00:19:26.051
"to myself I guess,

00:19:26.051 --> 00:19:29.023
"'cause she's giving it to me."

00:19:29.023 --> 00:19:31.785
And that's how I took my really long

00:19:31.785 --> 00:19:33.457
four and a half mile walk.

00:19:33.457 --> 00:19:36.175
And then you sat on the steps and weeped.

00:19:36.175 --> 00:19:38.309
Was that yesterday?
Yeah, mm-hmm.

00:19:38.309 --> 00:19:40.500
That was last night and---

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:44.407
Wept, I think is the word.
All I had to say,

00:19:44.407 --> 00:19:45.818
I don't have,

00:19:45.818 --> 00:19:49.683
I mean, I'm just saying I
relate to what you're saying.

00:19:49.683 --> 00:19:51.820
And all I can do is be grateful for,

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:54.148
because not everybody has that.

00:19:54.148 --> 00:19:56.815
Not everybody, like most people,

00:19:57.818 --> 00:20:00.099
and I'm not saying we don't
have frustrations at work

00:20:00.099 --> 00:20:01.349
from time to time,

00:20:01.349 --> 00:20:05.533
but it's an exceptional
thing to be like, man,

00:20:05.533 --> 00:20:08.450
they just did all this stuff for us

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:11.770
and the level of ownership,

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.893
I think that's the thing
that is so impressive

00:20:13.893 --> 00:20:17.282
with so many members of our
team is that they're like,

00:20:17.282 --> 00:20:20.061
this isn't just Rhett and Link saying,

00:20:20.061 --> 00:20:24.294
this is our thing and
we're gonna do it well,

00:20:24.294 --> 00:20:26.380
like we're gonna do it in an excellent way

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:29.297
and it just makes it fun, you know?

00:20:30.228 --> 00:20:31.728
Yeah and I mean,

00:20:32.731 --> 00:20:35.908
so many people have it so difficult.

00:20:35.908 --> 00:20:39.346
I mean, so many people don't
even have jobs to go to.

00:20:39.346 --> 00:20:40.988
So it's, you know--
A lot of people.

00:20:40.988 --> 00:20:43.988
Again, I want to acknowledge that.

00:20:45.251 --> 00:20:48.692
And just if there's any
way to, from my experience,

00:20:48.692 --> 00:20:50.892
I guess the encouragement,

00:20:50.892 --> 00:20:53.138
and I don't know if there's much to it

00:20:53.138 --> 00:20:55.707
is that if there's not normal in your life

00:20:55.707 --> 00:20:58.374
is there's a way to simulate it.

00:20:59.614 --> 00:21:02.107
I don't even know what that would mean.

00:21:02.107 --> 00:21:05.690
Even just a glimpse of
it for a few minutes

00:21:07.309 --> 00:21:09.264
helps so much.

00:21:09.264 --> 00:21:12.456
Okay, before we get into the questions,

00:21:12.456 --> 00:21:14.230
let's make it a little plug.

00:21:15.537 --> 00:21:18.687
Let's plug it up, plug action.

00:21:18.687 --> 00:21:21.520
We like to groom ourselves and,

00:21:22.793 --> 00:21:25.317
like a couple of chimpanzees.

00:21:25.317 --> 00:21:26.986
Well, we just groom each other.

00:21:26.986 --> 00:21:29.212
We groom ourselves.
No, we don't.

00:21:29.212 --> 00:21:31.856
And we have grooming products
available at mythical.com.

00:21:31.856 --> 00:21:33.364
You can see the Link
holding them right there.

00:21:33.364 --> 00:21:36.633
We've got Mythical pomade
for all your hair needs.

00:21:36.633 --> 00:21:37.466
I'm basically out.

00:21:37.466 --> 00:21:39.236
I gonna get some more.

00:21:39.236 --> 00:21:41.073
Look at that.
Your hair's gotten too long.

00:21:41.073 --> 00:21:42.405
Oh, I'm wearing a hat.
To using more of it.

00:21:42.405 --> 00:21:45.106
I'm wearing a hat today
because it's too long.

00:21:45.106 --> 00:21:48.523
Yeah, there's that and Mythical lip balm,

00:21:49.401 --> 00:21:52.911
Link's Peculiarly Perfect
Peanut Butter Peppermint flavor

00:21:52.911 --> 00:21:55.589
is available along with
Rhett's Wood 'N' Berries.

00:21:55.589 --> 00:21:57.769
I think we still have some of that.

00:21:57.769 --> 00:22:00.112
This particular one, it says Link's on it,

00:22:00.112 --> 00:22:00.945
'cause that's on the label,

00:22:00.945 --> 00:22:05.278
but then it also has a
printed label that says Link.

00:22:07.222 --> 00:22:09.092
Because it's yours and it's yours?

00:22:09.092 --> 00:22:11.199
It's mine and it is mine.

00:22:11.199 --> 00:22:12.056
That's right.

00:22:12.056 --> 00:22:13.676
This came from the office

00:22:13.676 --> 00:22:16.843
where I think it was in my makeup bag.

00:22:19.323 --> 00:22:21.422
Mythical.com.
My kit.

00:22:21.422 --> 00:22:22.839
Groom yourself.

00:22:24.337 --> 00:22:28.591
People always say that
they're pleasantly surprised

00:22:28.591 --> 00:22:32.666
'cause they think it's
just some sort of a gimmick

00:22:32.666 --> 00:22:34.175
that we sell lip balm,

00:22:34.175 --> 00:22:37.859
but I'll put this lip balm up
against anybody's lip balm.

00:22:37.859 --> 00:22:40.795
You will be pleasantly surprised

00:22:40.795 --> 00:22:43.367
because it's peculiarly perfect.

00:22:43.367 --> 00:22:46.284
Get it in a two pack, mythical.com.

00:22:48.205 --> 00:22:52.978
Okay, let's start with a
question from Michelle,

00:22:52.978 --> 00:22:55.774
@miss_belvedere on the Twitter.

00:22:55.774 --> 00:22:57.072
Mm-hmm.

00:22:57.072 --> 00:22:59.347
"What's your most irrational fear?

00:22:59.347 --> 00:23:02.650
"I am oddly afraid of wind turbines,

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:05.463
"and other large things, and bones."

00:23:05.463 --> 00:23:07.903
Wind turbines, wind turbine?

00:23:07.903 --> 00:23:09.044
How do you say that?

00:23:09.044 --> 00:23:11.154
She told me like a
windmill, like the ones out,

00:23:11.154 --> 00:23:14.159
going out to Palm Springs,
the huge windmills.

00:23:14.159 --> 00:23:16.221
Those are--
Yeah.

00:23:16.221 --> 00:23:19.144
I mean, you drive by those on the freeway

00:23:19.144 --> 00:23:21.073
if you're going out to Palm Springs,

00:23:21.073 --> 00:23:23.681
I remember the first time
I drove out to Palm Springs

00:23:23.681 --> 00:23:26.098
it was when we were going to,

00:23:26.993 --> 00:23:31.490
we were going to the
wrestlers reality house.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:32.620
I can't remember what it was called--

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:34.278
Legends' house.
Legends' house.

00:23:34.278 --> 00:23:35.666
They had a reality show

00:23:35.666 --> 00:23:38.383
and they brought us in as guest judges

00:23:38.383 --> 00:23:42.506
'cause the wrestlers
divided up into two teams

00:23:42.506 --> 00:23:44.756
and made local commercials.

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:48.855
We got to meet Rowdy Roddy Piper,

00:23:48.855 --> 00:23:51.344
who's may he rest in peace.

00:23:51.344 --> 00:23:53.226
Main Gene, may he rest in peace.

00:23:53.226 --> 00:23:55.059
Mean Gene, cool guy.

00:23:55.936 --> 00:23:58.812
That guy, man, you just got a sense

00:23:58.812 --> 00:24:00.123
this guy was full of stories.

00:24:00.123 --> 00:24:02.534
I wish we could have
hung out with him more.

00:24:02.534 --> 00:24:03.701
Hillbilly Jim.

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:07.097
Hacksaw Jim Duggan.
Was there?

00:24:07.097 --> 00:24:08.805
No, Hacksaw was there,

00:24:08.805 --> 00:24:13.611
who's one of Christy's
favorites for some reason.

00:24:13.611 --> 00:24:16.013
But I remember that was the
first time we went out to,

00:24:16.013 --> 00:24:19.702
we went to Palm Springs
and they put us in a,

00:24:19.702 --> 00:24:21.202
it wasn't a limo, it was a car.

